"Loopy: Low-Bit Quantization Framework for Looped Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.05265v1)\nciteturn41352view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.05265v1\",\"lineno\":237}); Total lines: 700\nL205:  | $$\\operatorname{Reg}_{T}(\\hat{c}_{T})\\leq 2\\max_{r<R}\\varepsilon_{r}.$$  |  | (12)\nL206: \nL207: Full-depth screening removes depth mismatch, leaving window-estimation error. This conditional bound applies to the fixed calibration set. Our schedule does not certify $\\varepsilon_{r}$, and the bound guarantees neither held-out generalization nor superiority at equal compute. Appendix cite41†C.4 proves the regret bound, and Appendix cite47†C.5 gives a sufficient condition for exact optimum recovery.\nL208: ## 4 Experiments\nL209: ### 4.1 Setup\nL210: Models and evaluation. We evaluate Ouro-1.4B and Ouro-2.6B at recurrent depth $T=4$ (cite61†Zhu et al., 2025 ), Huginn-0125 at $T=32$ (cite63†Geiping et al., 2025 ), and Recurrent-Llama-3.2-1B at $T=16$ (cite74†McLeish et al., 2025 ).\nL211: We report perplexity on WikiText-2 (cite82†Merity et al., 2016 ) and LAMBADA (cite83†Paperno et al., 2016 ), and zero-shot accuracy on HellaSwag (cite84†Zellers et al., 2019 ), WinoGrande (cite85†Sakaguchi et al., 2020 ), LAMBADA, ARC-Challenge (cite86†Clark et al., 2018 ), and MMLU (cite87†Hendrycks et al., 2021 ). Avg. denotes the unweighted mean of the five task accuracies.\nL212: Quantization and baselines. We evaluate W4A4 and W4A8 against recurrent adaptations of AWQ, SmoothQuant, QuaRot, SpinQuant, and FlatQuant (cite68†Lin et al., 2024 ; cite64†Xiao et al., 2023 ; cite65†Ashkboos et al., 2024 ; cite66†Liu et al., 2025 ; cite88†Sun et al., 2024 ). Loopy and all evaluated baselines uses symmetric group-32 quantization and symmetric per-token activation quantization. Baseline-specific quantization settings and calibration procedures are detailed in Appendix cite26†B .\nL213: LoopQ comparison uses the same Loopy results as the main table and published LoopQ results (cite70†Fang et al., 2026 ).\nL214: Calibration and selection. Loopy selects shared rotation and scaling configurations using progressive full-depth evaluation (Section cite10†3 ). Calibration bases remain fixed across candidates; nested window subsets and retained candidate counts are specified before search. All Loopy results in Table cite89†1 and the full-results appendix are obtained using configurations selected by the progressive full-depth procedure described in Section cite10†3 .\nL215: Implementation. Main accuracy results use quantization and dequantization in BF16 with KV caching disabled. We implement packed low-bit inference in Triton (cite90†Tillet et al., 2019 ), with hardware benchmarks described in Section cite19†4.5 .\nL216: ### 4.2 Main Results\nL217: \nL218: Table 1: W4A8 and W4A4 results on looped language models. WT2 and LMB denote WikiText-2 and LAMBADA perplexity ($\\downarrow$); Avg. is mean task accuracy (%, $\\uparrow$). Detailed accuracy results are shown in Tables cite91†5 and cite92†6 . Bold indicates the best low-bit result per model and precision.\nL219:  | Ouro-2.6B  | Ouro-1.4B  | Huginn-0125  | Recurrent-Llama-3.2-1B\nL220: Method  | WT2  | LMB  | Avg.  | WT2  | LMB  | Avg.  | WT2  | LMB  | Avg.  | WT2  | LMB  | Avg.\nL221: FP ref.  | 10.51  | 4.23  | 69.65  | 12.03  | 5.29  | 64.66  | 13.21  | 7.30  | 50.42  | 20.72  | 17.23  | 38.63\nL222: W4A8\nL223: AWQ  | 11.46  | 5.22  | 66.45  | 13.64  | 7.68  | 60.51  | 14.14  | 8.85  | 47.37  | 28.16  | 50.70  | 35.10\nL224: SmoothQuant  | 19.44  | 15.20  | 56.15  | 53.27  | 134.60  | 37.78  | —  | —  | 25.82  | 243.77  | —  | 26.40\nL225: QuaRot  | 11.76  | 5.30  | 66.76  | 14.25  | 8.29  | 59.09  | —  | —  | 25.61  | 26.03  | 31.15  | 36.60\nL226: SpinQuant  | 12.07  | 5.56  | 66.21  | 14.18  | 7.52  | 60.42  | —  | —  | 24.75  | 25.96  | 28.42  | 36.75\nL227: FlatQuant  | 14.52  | 8.14  | 62.42  | 26.55  | 23.50  | 46.21  | —  | —  | 24.43  | 86.73  | —  | 28.22\nL228: Loopy  | 10.97  | 4.46  | 68.64  | 12.77  | 6.00  | 63.00  | 13.68  | 7.98  | 49.21  | 24.76  | 26.97  | 37.23\nL229: W4A4\nL230: AWQ  | 340.35  | —  | 26.0  | 645.60  | —  | 25.6  | 168.39  | 117.12  | 34.2  | —  | —  | 25.0\nL231: SmoothQuant  | —  | —  | 24.5  | —  | —  | 25.2  | —  | —  | 25.3  | —  | —  | 25.5\nL232: QuaRot  | 15.27  | 9.73  | 56.9  | 19.16  | 22.18  | 44.7  | —  | —  | 25.3  | 48.08  | 139.25  | 31.5\nL233: SpinQuant  | 16.62  | 8.18  | 60.9  | 17.45  | 12.86  | 52.1  | —  | —  | 25.0  | 41.03  | 102.34  | 32.5\nL234: FlatQuant  | —  | —  | 24.6  | —  | —  | 25.0  | —  | —  | 25.0  | —  | —  | 25.1\nL235: Loopy  | 12.35  | 6.20  | 64.37  | 14.73  | 8.17  | 57.75  | 28.09  | 36.36  | 37.32  | 42.08  | 110.83  | 32.0\nL236: — denotes perplexity greater than $10^{3}$.\nL237: \nL238: W4A8. In Table cite89†1 , Loopy achieves the lowest WikiText-2 and LAMBADA perplexity and the highest available average accuracy among the evaluated low-bit methods on all four checkpoints.\nL239: W4A4. Loopy achieves the lowest perplexity on both datasets for the two Ouro checkpoints and Huginn-0125. Average accuracy exceeds the evaluated low-bit baselines on these three checkpoints. Recurrent-Llama exhibits greater degradation under W4A4 than W4A8, compared SpinQuant under W4A4, which is one of our work’s limitation.\nL240: Its inherited Llama pre-normalization structure differs from the normalization schemes used by Ouro and Huginn (cite74†McLeish et al., 2025 ; cite61†Zhu et al., 2025 ; cite63†Geiping et al., 2025 ), potentially affecting the propagation of quantization perturbations through recurrent execution.\nL241: Comparison with LoopQ. All LoopQ results are taken from its original paper (cite70†Fang et al., 2026 ), while Loopy results are reused from Table cite89†1 . Table cite93†2 shows that Loopy achieves lower W4A4 WikiText-2 perplexity and higher five-task average accuracy on both Ouro checkpoints.\nL242: Table 2: W4A4 comparison with LoopQ. $\\dagger$: published results (cite70†Fang et al., 2026 ); Avg. Acc. averages the five tasks in Table cite89†1 .\nL243:  | WT2 PPL $\\downarrow$  | Avg. Acc. (%) $\\uparrow$\nL244: --- | --- | ---\nL245: Model  | LoopQ^{†}  | Loopy  | LoopQ^{†}  | Loopy\nL246: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL247: Ouro-1.4B  | 15.81  | 14.73  | 57.08  | 57.75\nL248: Ouro-2.6B  | 15.89  | 12.35  | 58.13  | 64.37\nL249: ### 4.3 Selection Quality–Calibration Cost Trade-off\nL250: We compare LOOPY with full-depth uniform selection at evaluation depth $T$, shallow progressive selection at $T/2$, and shallow-to-full selection with evaluation depths $T/4\\rightarrow T/2\\rightarrow T$. Experiments use Ouro-1.4B W4A4 ($T=4$) and Huginn-0125 W4A8 ($T=32$). Full-depth uniform selection allocates a finite calibration budget equally across candidates, while the other strategies allocate additional calibration windows according to their respective selection procedures.\nL251: We measure the end-to-end calibration cost of each strategy, including base calibration, candidate construction or restoration, candidate screening, and final model construction.\nL252: Within each model, candidate representations and quantization settings are fixed. We repeat selection using $N=10$ independently sampled calibration sets, with all strategies sharing the same ordered calibration data within each run. Each strategy is evaluated under multiple predefined calibration budgets, and the selected configurations are evaluated at deployment depth $T$ on held-out WikiText-2 using token-normalized perplexity with 2048-token windows for convenience.\nL253: Figure 6 presents the relationship between calibration cost and selection quality, where lower perplexity indicates better selected configurations. Figure cite94†6 shows that Loopy achieves a favorable quality–cost trade-off across both models. Compared with full-depth exhaustive search, LOOPY reaches comparable selection quality with substantially lower calibration cost. On Ouro-1.4B W4A4 and Huginn-0125 W4A8, LOOPY reduces end-to-end calibration cost by 4.2× and 3.5×, respectively.\nL254: cite95†Image: Refer to caption Figure 6: Selection quality–calibration cost trade-off. WikiText-2 perplexity versus calibration time for (a) Ouro-1.4B W4A4 ($T=4$) and (b) Huginn-0125 W4A8 ($T=32$). Black stars denote exhaustive-search references. Lower perplexity is better.\nL255: ### 4.4 KV Cache Quantization\nL256: \nL257: We evaluate 4-bit and 8-bit KV quantization on Ouro-1.4B at $T=4$ with fixed Loopy W4A4 and W4A8 configurations, optionally applying post-RoPE Q/K Hadamard transforms following SpinQuant (cite66†Liu et al., 2025 ). We report WikiText-2 perplexity and average accuracy over four downstream tasks, with the full evaluation protocol and per-task results provided in Appendix cite24†A.1 .\nL258: As shown in Table cite96†3 , Q/K Hadamard transforms reduce the relative perplexity increase from KV4 quantization from 6.56% to 3.41% under W4A4 and from 4.38% to 2.19% under W4A8. They also improve average accuracy by 1.40 percentage points over KV4 under W4A4.\nL259: Table 3: KV quantization results. $\\Delta$PPL is relative to BF16 KV within each W/A setting; Avg. Acc. averages four tasks. $H$ denotes the Q/K Hadamard transform.\nL260: W/A  | KV setting  | PPL $\\downarrow$  | $\\Delta$PPL (%)  | Avg. Acc. (%) $\\uparrow$\nL261: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL262: W4A4  | BF16 KV  | 11.4174  | —  | 54.5266\nL263:  | BF16 KV+$H$  | 11.4139  | $-0.03$  | 54.4818\nL264:  | KV8  | 11.4109  | $-0.06$  | 54.1423\nL265:  | KV4  | 12.1660  | $+6.56$  | 51.9853\nL266:  | KV4+$H$  | 11.8062  | $+3.41$  | 53.3883\nL267: W4A8  | BF16 KV  | 9.3425  | —  | 61.7154\nL268:  | BF16 KV+$H$  | 9.3422  | $-0.004$  | 61.7387\nL269:  | KV8  | 9.3457  | $+0.03$  | 61.9927\nL270:  | KV4  | 9.7513  | $+4.38$  | 61.4155\nL271:  | KV4+$H$  | 9.5470  | $+2.19$  | 61.4134\nL272: ### 4.5 Deployment Efficiency and Validation\nL273: Decoding throughput. We evaluate Ouro-1.4B at a recurrent depth of $T=4$ on an NVIDIA H20 GPU under W4A4 and W4A8 quantization. With batch size 4 and an input length of 2048 tokens, online Loopy achieves about 41 and 41 decoding tokens per second (tokens/s), respectively, compared with 35 and 35 tokens/s for the official SpinQuant implementation. Table cite97†4 summarizes the results, while Appendix cite50†D details the timing protocol and implementation differences.\nL274: Table 4: Decoding throughput on NVIDIA H20 (tokens/s). B/input denotes batch size and input length. Protocol details and additional configurations appear in Appendix cite50†D .\nL275: \nL276:  | W4A4  | W4A8\nL277: Method  | B1/128  | B4/2048  | B1/128  | B4/2048\nL278: QuaRot  | $9.08^{*}$  | $35.14^{*}$  | 8.80  | 34.75\nL279: SpinQuant  | $9.16^{*}$  | $35.85^{*}$  | 9.14  | 35.78\nL280: Loopy (online)  | 10.01  | 40.93  | 10.22  | 41.17\nL281: Loopy (offline)^{†}  | 11.21  | 45.45  | 11.37  | 45.83\nL282: \nL283:   * $\\dagger$\nL284: Fully absorbable transform configuration.\nL285: \nL286:   * $*$\nL287: \nL288: Numerical-mismatch diagnostic runs.\nL289: ## 5 Related Work\nL290: Looped language models. Looped Transformers increase effective depth through repeated application of shared parameters, as explored by Universal Transformers and subsequent looped language models (cite60†Dehghani et al., 2019 ; cite62†Saunshi et al., 2025 ; cite63†Geiping et al., 2025 ; cite61†Zhu et al., 2025 ; cite98†Jeddi et al., 2026 ).\nL291: Parameter sharing reduces the number of distinct weights required to represent a deep computation, motivating studies of memory-efficient recurrent architectures (cite99†Vendrell et al., 2026 ).\nL292: Post-training quantization. Existing methods reduce quantization error through weight reconstruction or channel scaling, as exemplified by GPTQ, AWQ, and SmoothQuant (cite100†Frantar et al., 2022 ; cite68†Lin et al., 2024 ; cite64†Xiao et al., 2023 ). Complementing these approaches, rotation-based methods redistribute outliers, with QuaRot using structured orthogonal transformations and SpinQuant learning rotations through an end-to-end quantization objective (cite65†Ashkboos et al., 2024 ; cite66†Liu et al., 2025 ).\nL293: Recurrence-aware optimization. Repeated execution of the recurrent core produces evolving hidden states, allowing quantization errors to propagate across recurrent steps. LoopQ addresses this problem through loop-aware transformations and trajectory-aware calibration (cite70†Fang et al., 2026 ).\nL294: ## 6 Conclusion\nL295: \nL296: We presented Loopy, a PTQ framework that targets looped language models. By selecting shared low-bit representations according to their final target-depth prediction loss, Loopy aligns quantization selection with recurrent deployment and makes this selection practical through progressive full-depth evaluation. Experiments show benefits varying across architectures.\nL297: ## AI Use Statement\nL298: \nL299: Generative AI tools were used to assist with manuscript organization, language editing, literature checking, and the development and execution of code for manuscript preparation, verification, and supplementary experiments. The authors take full responsibility for the accuracy and integrity of the paper, including its claims, experimental results, references, and supplementary materials.\nL300: ### Reproducibility statement\nL301: \nL302: The supplementary source includes the numerical inputs, figure-generation code, source hashes, and a claim-to-evidence ledger. Appendix cite26†B describes the evaluation protocol, while Appendix cite51†E documents the supporting records for quantization evaluation, diagnostic analysis, and packed-execution validation.\nL303: ## References\nL304:   * Ashkboos et al. (2024) Saleh Ashkboos, Amirkeivan Mohtashami, Maximilian L Croci, Bo Li, Pashmina Cameron, Martin Jaggi, Dan Alistarh, Torsten Hoefler, and James Hensman. Quarot: Outlier-free 4-bit inference in rotated llms. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 37:100213–100240, 2024.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nORCA: The Annealed Spectral ConditioningOptimizer for Faster, Better LLM Training (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.06116v1)\nciteturn41352view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.06116v1\",\"lineno\":70}); Total lines: 498\nL59: On the cost side, however, imposing spectral constraints limits model expressiveness and can raise the attainable loss floor. Correspondingly, controlled interventions during Llama training empirically support both effects. Together, these results point to a seemingly trade-off between faster optimization and model expressiveness.\nL60: However, we argue that this trade-off is not fundamental. Spectral regularization may be most valuable early, when it can shape the optimization trajectory, yet become a liability if maintained as the weights adapt to the task. The two effects need not be accepted together: we can use the constraint to shape the spectrum, then release it before it restricts the final solution.\nL61: This motivates a minimal method ORCA (Orthogonal Regularization, Cooled After): During an initial phase, ORCA applies a soft orthogonality penalty, $R_{\\mathrm{orth}}(\\mathbf{W})=\\frac{1}{4}\\|\\mathbf{W}\\mathbf{W}^{\\top}-\\mathbf{I}\\|_{F}^{2}$. Then directly removes it at a single switch point. Training thereafter follows the standard optimizer update, with no gradual decay or further changes.\nL62: This design lets early regularization influence the optimization path without prescribing the spectrum throughout training. In our experiments, this simple release is followed by faster loss reduction and a lower final validation loss than Muon.\nL63: cite39†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1.1: LLaMA 130M training dynamics. Effective rank is measured on a middle-layer $V$ projection; the dashed line marks regularization release.\nL64: Figure cite40†1.1 illustrates the transition. While the penalty is active, the effective rank of the weight matrix rises sharply above Muon’s, although ORCA initially has a higher validation loss. After release, the rank falls from its peak but remains above Muon’s, while validation loss decreases rapidly, crosses the Muon curve. The result is consistent with early spectral shaping leaving a useful effect on the subsequent trajectory, even after the explicit penalty is gone.\nL65: Controlled interventions in Section cite12†5 further investigates into this connection.\nL66: We evaluate ORCA on LLaMA, Qwen3, and fine-grained MoE models ranging from 130M to 8B parameters, including 100B-token training runs. ORCA achieves lower final validation loss than Muon across these settings. In most experiments, the reduction is 0.02–0.04, comparable to or greater than Muon’s gain over Adam. To put this loss reduction in perspective, on LLaMA 1.3B, ORCA achieving the 0.024 lower validation loss reaches Muon’s 100k-step loss in just 60k steps.\nL67: Further, downstream evaluations, alternative early spectral interventions, multi-seed runs, and ablations of method parameters and intervention timing further support these gains. Together, these results show that early spectral shaping followed by constraint relaxation can improve training efficiency and final performance across architectures without substantial changes to the optimizer.\nL68: To summarize the contributions of the paper: (1) We illustrate a trade-off in spectral control through a simplified model and empirical observations: favorable spectra can accelerate optimization but persistent constraints may harm the attainable solution quality. (2) We introduce ORCA, a minimal shape-then-release optimizer that leverages early orthogonal regularization to improve optimization trajectories while allowing unconstrained adaptation in later training.\nL69: (3) We demonstrate ORCA’s effectiveness across diverse LLM architectures and scales, achieving faster convergence and lower loss than existing optimizers. Through extensive controlled experiments, we further validate the mechanism that early effective-rank shaping drives the observed improvements.\nL70: ## 2 Spectral Structure and Optimization\nL71: \nL72: As noted in Section 1, Muon’s higher effective rank coincides with faster optimization. This observation suggests that properties of the weight spectrum may be useful for optimization, but whether they provide a general benefit remains unclear. To turn this still-vague possibility into a precise question about optimization dynamics, we ask:\nL73: \nL74: Q1. Can the spectral structure observed under Muon directly affect optimization speed, and through what mechanism?\nL75: A direct mechanism already appears in ’the multiplicatively coupled weight matrices’ of Transformers. Consider for example the value–output pair $\\mathbf{W}_{V}\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ of a single attention head:\nL76: \nL77:  | $$\\operatorname{Attn}(\\mathbf{H})=\\operatorname{softmax}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{W}_{Q}\\mathbf{W}_{K}^{\\top}\\mathbf{H}^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d_{h}}}\\right)\\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{W}_{V}\\mathbf{W}_{O}.$$  |  | (2.1)\nL78: \nL79: The gradient of the value projection takes the form\nL80:  | $$\\nabla_{\\mathbf{W}_{V}}L=\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\\top},\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\\mathbf{G}=\\nabla_{(\\mathbf{W}_{V}\\mathbf{W}_{O})}L.$$  |\nL81: Here, $\\mathbf{G}$ denotes the gradient with respect to the entire product $\\mathbf{W}_{V}\\mathbf{W}_{O}$. Thus, $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ acts as a spectral filter on the gradient received by $\\mathbf{W}_{V}$. Writing $\\mathbf{W}_{O}=\\sum_{i}\\sigma_{i}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}$, we have $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{W}_{V}}L=\\sum_{i}\\sigma_{i}(\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{v}_{i})\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}$.\nL82: This makes the role of the spectrum explicit: each component $\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{v}_{i}$ enters the gradient with weight $\\sigma_{i}$. If $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ is scaled orthogonal, $\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\\top}$ preserves the spectrum of $\\mathbf{G}$ up to a common scale. At the opposite extreme, if $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ has rank $r$, the update is restricted to contributions from $r$ spectral directions.\nL83: More generally, as the spectrum becomes more concentrated, directions associated with smaller singular values contribute proportionally less to the update.\nL84: In fact, this partner-dependent filtering is ubiquitous in Transformers: analogous coupling appears between the query–key pair $(\\mathbf{W}_{Q},\\mathbf{W}_{K})$ and the up- and down-projections of MLP and expert blocks.\nL85: To isolate this mechanism from the surrounding Transformer computation, consider the generic factorization $\\mathbf{W}=\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{Y}$, where $\\mathbf{G}=\\nabla_{\\mathbf{W}}L(\\mathbf{W})$. The simple chain rule gives $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{X}}L=\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{Y}^{\\top},\\nabla_{\\mathbf{Y}}L=\\mathbf{X}^{\\top}\\mathbf{G},$ so each factor directly filters the gradient received by its partner. Under the continuous-time exact-polar Muon dynamics (cite41†Ambrosio et al., 2005 ; cite42†Peyré, 2026 ),\nL86:  | $$\\dot{\\mathbf{X}}=-\\eta_{t}^{X}\\operatorname{Polar}(\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{Y}^{\\top}),\\qquad\\dot{\\mathbf{Y}}=-\\eta_{t}^{Y}\\operatorname{Polar}(\\mathbf{X}^{\\top}\\mathbf{G}),$$  |\nL87: \nL88: where $\\operatorname{Polar}(\\mathbf{M})=\\mathbf{U}\\mathbf{V}^{\\top}$ for an SVD $\\mathbf{M}=\\mathbf{U}\\mathbf{\\Sigma}\\mathbf{V}^{\\top}$. The instantaneous loss decrease is therefore\nL89: \nL90:  | $$-\\dot{L}=\\eta_{t}^{X}\\left\\|\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{Y}^{\\top}\\right\\|_{*}+\\eta_{t}^{Y}\\left\\|\\mathbf{X}^{\\top}\\mathbf{G}\\right\\|_{*}.$$  |\nL91: Thus, the same filtered gradients above are the quantities that determine Muon’s instantaneous loss decrease. The local spectral effect can therefore translate into a local effect on optimization speed.\nL92: To empirically demonstrate this effect, we conduct a reciprocal $V$–$O$ intervention. Let $\\mathbf{G}_{O}=\\nabla_{\\mathbf{W}_{O}}L$ denote the gradient with respect to $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$, and let $\\mathbf{U}_{O}=\\operatorname{Polar}(\\mathbf{G}_{O})$ denote the corresponding Muon update direction.\nL93: At a 10k-step checkpoint, we raise the smaller singular values of $\\mathbf{W}_{V}$ toward its largest singular value while keeping its singular vectors and spectral norm fixed, and update only $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ with Muon.\nL94: As shown in cite43†Figure 1(b) , with the Muon update scale $\\|\\mathbf{U}_{O}\\|_{F}$ held fixed, increasing the effective rank of $\\mathbf{W}_{V}$ increases $\\|\\mathbf{G}_{O}\\|_{*}$—the loss decrease under Muon flow—while decreasing the gradient–weight alignment $\\cos(\\mathbf{G}_{O},\\mathbf{W}_{O})$. These all indicate that the same-sized Muon update has a stronger effect as the effective rank of $\\mathbf{W}_{V}$ increases.\nL95: Consistently, cite44†Figure 1(c) also shows that updating $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ yields a larger held-out loss decrease at higher effective rank of $\\mathbf{W}_{V}$.\nL96: cite45†Image: Refer to caption (a) Absolute singular-value spectra.\nL97: \nL98: (b) Gradient and update metrics.\nL99: \nL100: (c) Held-out loss decrease.\nL101: \nL102: Figure 2.1: Single-layer $V$-spectrum intervention with $O$-only Muon updates. $\\mathbf{G}_{O}$ and $\\mathbf{U}_{O}$ denote the gradient and Muon update direction. In (c), rollout steps and loss decrease use logarithmic scales.\nL103: These observations suggest a simple principle: spectral concentration can create an optimization bottleneck. For a nonzero matrix $\\mathbf{A}$, define its effective rank by $\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{A}):=\\left\\|\\mathbf{A}\\right\\|_{F}^{2}/\\left\\|\\mathbf{A}\\right\\|_{2}^{2}$. To make this intuition concrete, we study a matrix-factorization model. The following bounds illustrate one possible mathematical pathway through which spectral structure may affect optimization.\nL104: ###### Theorem 2.1 (Low Effective Rank Limits Descent Speed).\nL105: For $k\\geq 2$, let $\\mathbf{X}$ and $\\mathbf{Y}$ be conformable matrices with $\\mathbf{W}=\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{Y}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k\\times k}$, and consider the column-wise softmax loss $$L(\\mathbf{W})=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\left[\\log\\!\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^{k}e^{\\mathbf{W}_{ji}}\\right)-\\mathbf{W}_{c_{i}i}\\right],\\quad c_{i}\\in[k],\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\\mathbf{W}=\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{Y}$$ (2.2) let $(\\mathbf{X}_{t},\\mathbf{Y}_{t})_{t\\in[0,T]}$ be an absolutely continuous solution of the exact-polar Muon flow above, with $\\eta_{0}\\geq 0$ and $0\\leq\\eta_{t}^{X}\\leq\\eta_{0}/\\left\\|\\mathbf{Y}_{t}\\right\\|_{2},\\qquad 0\\leq\\eta_{t}^{Y}\\leq\\eta_{0}/\\left\\|\\mathbf{X}_{t}\\right\\|_{2}.$ Assume that the factor norms satisfy $\\left\\|\\mathbf{X}_{t}\\right\\|_{2},\\left\\|\\mathbf{Y}_{t}\\right\\|_{2}>0$ for all $t\\in[0,T]$.\nL106: Writing $L_{t}=L(\\mathbf{X}_{t}\\mathbf{Y}_{t})$, we have $$L_{T}\\geq L_{0}\\exp\\!\\left[-\\sqrt{2}\\,\\eta_{0}\\int_{0}^{T}\\left(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{X}_{s})}+\\sqrt{\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{Y}_{s})}\\right)ds\\right].$$\nL107: cite46†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2.2: Validation loss under persistent spectral constraints.\nL108: In this factorization model, the bound relates the rate of loss decrease to the effective ranks of the factors. Naturally, broad spectral support may alleviate this bottleneck, as demonstrated by the intervention experiments in cite47†Figure 2.1 . We therefore test whether maintaining such support throughout LLaMA pretraining helps by imposing progressively stronger spectral constraints. However, cite48†Figure 2.2 shows that persistent orthogonal constraints worsen validation loss.\nL109: Thus, the path-side advantage identified above does not translate into a benefit when the same structure is enforced persistently. This leads to our second question:\nL110: Q2. If broad spectral structure alleviates an optimization bottleneck, why enforcing it hurts performance?\nL111: The key is that a property that is favorable along the optimization trajectory need not be favorable when imposed as a persistent constraint. Spectral control does more than influence how an optimizer moves: when enforced throughout training, it also restricts the spectral structure of the solutions that the optimizer is allowed to reach. For example, an orthogonality constraint $\\mathbf{W}\\mathbf{W}^{\\top}\\approx\\mathbf{I}$ forces the singular values of $\\mathbf{W}$ to remain nearly equal.\nL112: If the task prefers a more anisotropic or lower-effective-rank solution, such a constraint can prevent the optimizer from reaching it.\nL113: The factorized model above makes this competing effect concrete. For the same column-wise softmax objective in Eq. (cite49†2.2 ), the following theorem shows that sufficiently high effective rank can itself impose a nonzero loss floor.\nL114: ###### Theorem 2.2 (High Effective Rank Can Impose a Loss Floor).\nL115: Assume $k\\geq 2$, $\\mathbf{W}\\neq 0$, and $\\|\\mathbf{W}\\|_{2}\\leq B$ for a fixed scale bound $B=O(1)$. Let $K=\\left|\\{c_{i}:i\\in[k]\\}\\right|$ denote the number of distinct target indices. Then $$L(\\mathbf{W})\\geq C_{k}(\\mathbf{W})\\,k\\left(1-\\sqrt{\\frac{K}{\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{W})}}\\right),$$ where $C_{k}(\\mathbf{W})>0$ is the scale factor determined by $k$ and $\\|\\mathbf{W}\\|_{F}/\\sqrt{k}$.\n"
